%N A397526 Number of ways to write 6*n as p + q, where p and q are primes such that 2*p+1 and 4*q+1 are also prime.
%C A397526 For p > 3, 2*p+1 prime forces p == 2 (mod 3), and for q > 3, 4*q+1 prime forces q == 1 (mod 3); hence p + q is a multiple of 6 whenever p, q > 3, which is why only multiples of 6 are considered.
%C A397526 Conjecture: a(n) > 0 for all n > 0.
%C A397526 The conjecture implies that there are infinitely many Sophie Germain primes (A005384) and infinitely many primes q with 4*q+1 prime (A023212): if either set were finite, the other would contain m - s for some fixed s for a positive proportion of multiples m of 6, contradicting the fact that the primes have density zero.
%C A397526 This has been verified for all n <= 22906492166.
%e A397526 a(1) = 1 since 6 = 3 + 3 with 3, 3, 7 and 13 all prime.
%e A397526 a(3) = 2 since 18 = 11 + 7 with 11, 7, 23 and 29 all prime; 18 = 5 + 13 with 5, 13, 11 and 53 all prime.
%t A397526 a[n_] := With[{m = 6 n}, Count[Prime[Range[PrimePi[m - 2]]], q_ /; PrimeQ[4 q + 1] && PrimeQ[m - q] && PrimeQ[2 (m - q) + 1]]]; Table[a[n], {n, 1, 87}]
%o A397526 (PARI) a(n) = my(m=6*n, c=0); forprime(q=2, m-2, if(isprime(4*q+1) && isprime(m-q) && isprime(2*(m-q)+1), c++)); c
%o A397526 (Python)
%o A397526 from sympy import isprime, primerange
%o A397526 def a(n):
%o A397526     m = 6*n
%o A397526     return sum(1 for q in primerange(2, m - 1) if isprime(4*q+1) and isprime(m - q) and isprime(2*(m - q)+1))
%Y A397526 Cf. A000040, A005384, A023212, A002375, A227923.
